meta {
  name: 60 Referral Status & Delete (E2E)
  seq: 60
}

docs {
  End-to-end flow for the **referral Active/Inactive toggle + delete-in-use guard**
  (applies to all four referral types — this flow uses **External Referral** as the
  representative; Panel/Doctor/Internal behave identically, except **Referral Panel**
  toggles `isActive` instead of `status`).

  Run the requests **in order** (01 → 11). Fully self-contained — needs only a valid
  business login on `{{baseUrl}}`. What it proves:

  - Toggling a referral **Inactive** (`PATCH … { status: INACTIVE }`) is persisted
    and **non-destructive** — the record stays in the full listing (step 07) but
    disappears from `GET /external-referrals/options` (step 06), the endpoint that
    feeds the `/registration/create` dropdowns. Reactivating restores it (steps 08–09).
  - Each list row carries **`hasActiveOrder`** (step 04) — `true` when the referral is
    used by an order still in an active accessioning/technician-stage workflow.
  - With no active order, delete succeeds (step 10) and the record 404s (step 11).

  ## Delete-in-use guard (409) — behaviour reference

  When the referral **is** used by an active-workflow order — a non-cancelled order
  (`status ≠ CANCELLED`, not soft-deleted) that is still mid-workflow: **either** a
  sample in an in-flight accessioning state (`NEW`/`COLLECTED`/`HOLD`/`REPEAT`/`HALT`/
  `SENT_INTERNAL`/`FORWARD_EXTERNAL`/`OUTSOURCED`) **or** a lab report not yet
  `PUBLISHED` — the list row reports `hasActiveOrder: true` and:

      DELETE {{baseUrl}}/external-referrals/{{id}}
      → 409  { "success": false,
               "error": { "code": "EXTERNAL_REFERRAL_IN_USE",
                          "message": "This external referral cannot be deleted because it is used by an active order" } }

  Per type the code is `REFERRAL_PANEL_IN_USE` / `REFERRAL_DOCTOR_IN_USE` /
  `INTERNAL_REFERRAL_IN_USE`. Deactivate instead (`PATCH { status: INACTIVE }`).
  Cancelling the order (or publishing all its reports) clears `hasActiveOrder` and the
  delete then succeeds. **To reproduce:** attach `{{flowReferralId}}` to an order via
  the **47 Order Flow (E2E)** setup (add `"externalReferralId": "{{flowReferralId}}"`
  to *25 Create Order*), then run *10 Delete* — it returns 409 until you cancel the order.

  Captured var: `flowReferralId`.
}
